Q: how to create a bokeh colormapper in javascript? A: <p>After reading Bokeh docs and js source this seems to work with version 0.12.5</p>
<pre><code>var color_mapper = new Bokeh.LogColorMapper({palette:'Viridis256', low:0, high:16});
</code></pre>
<p>I did try to get some sane result in example below but without much success. Maybe you can improve this code further. This is based on python source from <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/43212721/updating-color-mapper-in-a-bokeh-plot-with-taptool#43214378">Updating color mapper in a bokeh plot with taptool</a>
Try to click on button &quot;Add some data!&quot; many times, more then 10.</p>
<pre><code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html&gt;
&lt;head&gt;
  &lt;meta http-equiv=&quot;content-type&quot; content=&quot;text/html; charset=UTF-8&quot;&gt;
  &lt;meta name=&quot;robots&quot; content=&quot;noindex, nofollow&quot;&gt;
  &lt;meta name=&quot;googlebot&quot; content=&quot;noindex, nofollow&quot;&gt;
      &lt;script type=&quot;text/javascript&quot; src=&quot;https://cdn.bokeh.org/bokeh/release/bokeh-0.12.5.min.css&quot; type=&quot;text/css&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
      &lt;script type=&quot;text/javascript&quot; src=&quot;https://cdn.bokeh.org/bokeh/release/bokeh-0.12.5.min.js&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
      &lt;script type=&quot;text/javascript&quot; src=&quot;https://cdn.bokeh.org/bokeh/release/bokeh-api-0.12.5.min.js&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
  &lt;title&gt; by bokeh&lt;/title&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;
&lt;body&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
&lt;div id=&quot;myplot&quot; /&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;button onclick=&quot;addPoint()&quot;&gt;Add some data!&lt;/button&gt;

&lt;script type='text/javascript'&gt;//&lt;![CDATA[
// arrays to hold data
var source = new Bokeh.ColumnDataSource({
  data: { x: [Math.random() * 10.0], y: [Math.random() * 10.0], humidity: [0, 10.0] }
});
var color_mapper = new Bokeh.LogColorMapper({palette:'Viridis256', low:0, high:16});

// make the plot and add some tools
var tools = &quot;pan,crosshair,wheel_zoom,box_zoom,reset,save&quot;;

var plot = Bokeh.Plotting.figure({title:'Example of Random data', tools: tools, height: 300, width: 300});

var pglyph = plot.patches({ field: &quot;x&quot; }, { field: &quot;y&quot; },
    { fill_color: { field: &quot;humidity&quot;, transform: color_mapper}},
                   { source: source, alpha: 1, line_width: 4})

var scatterData = plot.line({ field: &quot;x&quot; }, { field: &quot;y&quot; },
    { source: source, line_width: 10 });

// Show the plot, appending it to the end of the current
// section of the document we are in.
Bokeh.Plotting.show(plot, document.getElementById('myplot'));

function addPoint() {
  // The data can be added, but generally all fields must be the
  // same length.
  source.data.x.push(Math.random() * 10.0);
  source.data.y.push(Math.random() * 10.0);
  // Also, the DataSource object must be notified when it has changed.
  source.trigger('change');
}
//]]&gt;
&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: What is the problem namespaces in XML trying to solve? A: <ol>
<li><p>Browsers only parse HTML, SVG and MathML. The HTML specification has special rules for how to parse SVG and MathML in HTML (and lots of special rules as to how to parse HTML itself). If we wanted to add some other thing then we'd have to all agree what the rules are for parsing it.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Parsing of XML is entirely generic. A parser does not need to know any context special rules. Nobody has to form a committee to determine what rules to use to parse anything else.</p>
</li>
<li><p>XML came before SVG/MathML parsing in HMTL. Really because XHTML never really took off in browsers.</p>
</li>
<li><p>XSD can work with any XML containing non-XHTML, non-SVG and non-MathML data. HTML can only contain those 3 things.</p>
</li>
<li><p>XML is used for lots of things that have nothing to do with browsers and where it contains no HTML, SVG or MathML at all.</p>
</li>
<li><p>A concrete example would be anything other than SVG or MathML within HTML. The parser wouldn't know how to parse it so it would treat it all as unknown HTML elements which may not be what you wanted.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>In terms of parsing let's say you have data about dogs in some standard form.</p>
<pre><code>&lt;dog breed=&quot;terrier&quot;...&gt;
   &lt;bark pitch=&quot;&quot; duration=&quot;&quot;/&gt;
&lt;/dog&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>and other data about trees</p>
<pre><code>&lt;tree species=&quot;pine&quot; ...&gt;
   &lt;bark texture=&quot;smooth&quot; colour=&quot;brown&quot;.../&gt;
&lt;/tree&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>and we want to extend our dog dataset to contain information about which kind of tree each breed of dog likes best.</p>
<pre><code>&lt;dog breed=&quot;terrier&quot;...&gt;
   &lt;bark pitch=&quot;&quot; duration=&quot;&quot;/&gt;
   &lt;likes&gt;
     &lt;tree species=&quot;pine&quot; ...&gt;
       &lt;bark texture=&quot;smooth&quot; colour=&quot;brown&quot;.../&gt;
     &lt;/tree&gt;
   &lt;/likes&gt;
&lt;/dog&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Now the meaning of bark depends on the context i.e. what its parent is rather than having a single fixed meaning throughout the file.</p>
<p>Namespaces mark that context change. The two bark elements would be in different namespaces use that to validate all the attributes of the different bark elements.</p>

Q: HTML5 Canvas: How to generate ambigram-style text same after 180&#176; rotation? A: <p>You are looking for a way to to find the center (x, y) of the text. Luckily, there's <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/CanvasRenderingContext2D/measureText" rel="nofollow noreferrer">measureText</a> method which allows to get the dimensions of the text. It returns a <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/TextMetrics" rel="nofollow noreferrer">TextMetrics</a> that contains the info although a little more complicated than <code>getBoundingclientRect</code>.</p>
<p>Please note the text is aligned to the left, with the default baseline.</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const canvas = document.createElement('canvas');
document.body.append(canvas)
const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');
canvas.width = 320;
canvas.height = 200;

ctx.font = "48px arial";
ctx.textAlign = "left"
const text = "NOON";
const centerX = canvas.width / 2;
const centerY = canvas.height / 2;
var angle = 0;

function draw() {
  ctx.clearRect(0, 0, canvas.width, canvas.height);

  ctx.save();
  const m = ctx.measureText(text);

  // see https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/TextMetrics#measuring_text_width
  const width = m.actualBoundingBoxLeft + m.actualBoundingBoxRight;
  const height = m.actualBoundingBoxAscent + m.actualBoundingBoxDescent;

  const anchorX = centerX - (width / 2);
  const anchorY = centerY + (height) / 2;

  ctx.strokeStyle = 'lightblue';
  ctx.lineWidth = 1;
  ctx.strokeRect(
    anchorX - m.actualBoundingBoxLeft,
    anchorY - m.actualBoundingBoxAscent,
    width,
    height
  );

  ctx.fillStyle = 'red';
  ctx.beginPath();
  ctx.arc(anchorX, anchorY, 4, 0, Math.PI * 2);
  ctx.fill();

  // first text
  ctx.fillStyle = 'green';
  ctx.fillText(text, anchorX, anchorY);

  // move to center
  const newCenterX = anchorX - m.actualBoundingBoxLeft + width / 2
  const newCenterY = anchorY - m.actualBoundingBoxAscent + height / 2

  ctx.translate(newCenterX, newCenterY)
  ctx.fillStyle = 'red';
  ctx.beginPath();
  ctx.arc(0, 0, 4, 0, Math.PI * 2);
  ctx.fill();

  ctx.rotate(angle)

  // translate back
  ctx.translate(-newCenterX, -newCenterY)

  // the new text (yellow over green!)
  ctx.fillStyle = 'yellow';
  ctx.fillText(text, anchorX, anchorY);

  ctx.restore()

  angle += Math.PI / 180
  requestAnimationFrame(draw)
}

draw();</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>

Q: Destroy derived class from 2 downcasted references A: <p>Use composition over inheritance:</p>
<pre><code>struct Data {};

struct E  {
    Data&amp; data;
    E(Data&amp; data) : data(data) {}
};

struct A : public E {};
struct B : public E {};

struct EE {
    Data data;
    A x{data};
    B y{data};
    E&amp; a() { return x; }
    E&amp; b() { return y; }
};
</code></pre>
<p>The question you ask is actually unclear. You say you store references to <code>E</code> in a vector, but who owns the orginal <code>EE</code> instances where you got the references from?
Create them on the stack or store them in a container. The references you get from <code>a()</code> and <code>b()</code> need not and should not be used to destroy the acutal instances:</p>
<pre><code>{
    std::vector&lt;EE&gt; ee;
    {
       std::vector&lt;std::reference_wrapper&lt;E&gt;&gt; refs;
       // do something with refs 
    }
} // EE objects are destroyed
</code></pre>
<p>If you need the return values of <code>a()</code> and <code>b()</code> to participate in ownership, make them smart pointers. However, it does not seem to be necessary.</p>
<p>In terms of your added <code>main</code>:</p>
<pre><code>int main() {
  std::vector&lt;EE&gt; v;
  std::vector&lt;E*&gt; vec;

  for (size_t i = 0; i &lt; 10; ++i) {
    v.push_back({});
    vec.push_back(&amp;(v.back().x));
    vec.push_back(&amp;(v.back().y));
  }

  // shuffle the `vec`

  for (size_t i = 0; i &lt; 20; ++i) {
    // your code goes here
  }


}  // At this point v and all its elements are destroyed
</code></pre>
<p>I used a vector of pointers to <code>E</code> because its simpler. With reference wrappers its basically the same.</p>
